@page "/large-list"
@rendermode InteractiveServer

<PageTitle>Large List - Keyed Morph</PageTitle>

<h1>Large List (500 keyed rows)</h1>

<p>Trace of the <em>large-list</em> example (keyed morph): 500 linhas com chaves
    (<code>row-N</code>); the reorder resends the same rows in a different order and, with keys, only the
    moved nodes change position instead of re-rendering the whole table. In BRHC the keys
    are the <code>id</code>s on each <code>&lt;tr&gt;</code> and the morph happens on the
    <code>patch-elements</code>; aqui re-renderizo a lista em C# e o Blazor reconciliation
    compares the render tree - with <code>@@key</code>, it preserves the nodes (the keyed-morph analogue).
    <strong>Teaching note:</strong> it is the most direct comparison: two keyed diffs, one data model.
    um por atributos no documento (Datastar), outro pela render tree (Blazor).
</p>

<div class="demo-box">
    <div class="border rounded p-2" style="max-height: 24em; overflow-y: auto;">
        <table class="table table-sm table-hover">
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var row in rows)
                {
                    <tr @key="row.Id">
                        <td class="text-end" style="width: 6em;">@row.Id</td>
                        <td>@row.Label</td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <button class="btn btn-primary mt-3" @onclick="Reorder" disabled="@reordering">
        @(reordering ? "reordenando…" : "Reorder")
    </button>
</div>

@code {
    // 500 rows with stable keys - the property the keyed morph relies on.
    private sealed record Row(int Id, string Label);

    private List<Row> rows = Enumerable.Range(1, 500).Select(i => new Row(i, $"Row {i}")).ToList();
    private bool reordering;

    // Equivalent to the Kotlin GET /large-list/reorder: same list, reverse order;
    // with @@key Blazor moves the existing <tr> nodes (it does not recreate 500 nodes).
    private async Task Reorder()
    {
        reordering = true;
        StateHasChanged();
        await Task.Delay(50); // deixe o badge mostrar o estado pendente
        rows = rows.OrderByDescending(r => r.Id).ToList();
        reordering = false;
        StateHasChanged();
    }
}